  I happen to be an attorney. Jonadab is not entirely correct when
he's speaking of intellectual property. He's making conclusions about
what he thinks should be reasonable under the law instead of knowing
the letter of the law. This is very common. Reason and logic,
especially when it makes sense to an individual or his like minded
peers, is a dangerous red herring. The laws rarely conform to notions
of how one thinks it should be. They are set up by long processes that
take into account issues and points of view that no one person could
ever consider.

  And the laws are often just plain wrong and unfair. Immoral even.
Intellectual property is defended broadly and fiercely by the US and
international legal systems.

  As for the status of ownership/control of the Nethack name and code
I have no idea. Having read the license it still is not clear. It is
clear you can alter THE CODE anyway you like as long as you keep the
source open to others. It is not clear if the name is owned. Which is
the real issue here. The name.

  If I were the attorney for this group, and I'm NOT YOUR ATTORNEY
(legal disclaimers, love it!). But if I were I would recommend asking
for permission. If permission cannot be obtained for whatever reason I
recommend a simple name change. This would stave off any hurt
feelings, and hurt feelings are what drive legal action when no real
money is at stake.

  Nethack 4.K is my whimsical recommendation. Get it, Four-K, fork!
Not sure Nethack 4.0 goes far enough to prevent hurt feelings.

  It's important to remember that the letter of the law may say one
thing, but people drive legal action. It's not automatic.

  To take the name over without first asking the current Dev Team
would amount to a hostile take over. It is not the way to go.


